From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:02:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: send rumble and HDR metadata at once (control
 doorbell)

controlBroadcastThread drains the gamepad feedback queue (rumble,
trigger rumble, LEDs, motion event requests) and the HDR queue once per
loop and then waits in enet_host_service() for up to 150 ms. Without
input from the client only Moonlight's 100 ms ping wakes it, so rumble
reached the client up to ~100 ms (on average ~50 ms) after the game set
it, and HDR metadata likewise. ENet is not thread-safe, so the threads
that produce feedback (ViGEm notification callbacks, the encoder thread)
cannot send it themselves.

New module src/splitplay/doorbell.{h,cpp}: the control thread arms it
with the control port once its ENet host exists (the bind address, or
loopback for the wildcard; IPv6 loopback for a dual-stack host) and a
random 16-byte per-process token. ring(), called right after each
feedback_queue->raise() in platform/windows/input.cpp and after each HDR
raise in video.cpp, sends the token to that port once (an atomic flag
suppresses further datagrams until the control thread has woken; the
loop clears it before draining, so no wake-up is lost and a lost
datagram costs at most one upstream cycle). The ENet host's intercept
callback recognises the token, consumes the datagram and reports an
event without a peer, which ends enet_host_service(); iterate() returns
on such an event and the loop drains and sends the queues as upstream
does, on the control thread. Every other datagram goes to ENet as
before.

Rumble now leaves the host within the control thread's wake-up time
(well under 1 ms) instead of up to ~100 ms. One loopback datagram per
change of rumble or LED state; the loop itself is unchanged.

